														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4843343">
									

<p>Got a PM about this, took a look. I'll admit that the first few sentences of the description make me think of <a href="/scp-1836">SCP-1836</a> (Mother in the Ice), only this is kind of a less interesting version because 1836 incorporates the legendary figure Sedna. This iceberg just kind of&#8230; seemingly exists in a strangely specific way for no reason.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Over the course of the article, a journal is found from a monkey</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This just seems too conveniently story-wise. A lot of readers these days tend to be skeptical of the &quot;wow look how nice that someone left a document that explains everything and also spoonfeeds story exposition too!&quot; approach; if you really want to have the majority of the information about the item come from the documentation, a tale or GoI-format document may be a better approach. Otherwise the Foundation involvement seems like an afterthought, which wouldn't make sense for an SCP article about something seemingly so grandiose in terms of human implications.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>who was bestowed intelligence by the Avatar of the Earth. Our monkey scientist then grapples with the stupidity of mankind,</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm confused as to why the monkey scientist is so immediately dismissive of humans. We've got stuff like cloud seeding, painkillers, the internet&#8230; it also seems rather hypocritical to just see humans as a whole as &quot;stupid&quot; given that there are plenty of intelligent and good-hearted people out there.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>before realizing that the only way to protect what gave him the ground to stand on, is to pack everything away and create a failsafe, so that Earth may live on.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not sure what this means. What is the failsafe? What is being packed away? And if the monkey thinks humans are so stupid, why bother writing a journal that humans can understand?</p>
<p>Sorry, but I don't see this being workable at its current stage because all the individual elements seem kind of haphazardly smashed together rather than being thematically related in the context of the story. How is an iceberg related to turning people into monkeys, and how is that related to some random scientist dishing out their hot take on humanity? Why are shrines with feeling-language needed at all in this story?</p>

							</div>

								
					
