																	<a href="/forum/t-13909299/scp-idea:the-necro-matriarch-broadcast-manifestations#post-4843333">Re: SCP Idea: The Necro-Matriarch Broadcast Manifestations</a>
									</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/zyn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1404533); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1404533&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607826039" alt="Zyn" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1404533)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/zyn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1404533); return false;" >Zyn</a></span> <span class="odate time_1607819126 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">13 Dec 2020 00:25</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-89000/help:ideas-critique">Writing Help / Help: Ideas Critique</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13909299/scp-idea:the-necro-matriarch-broadcast-manifestations">SCP Idea: The Necro-Matriarch Broadcast Manifestations</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4843333">
									

<blockquote>
<p>For the ones who have a bad memory, no matter how severe it is, that same &quot;grandma&quot; will be completely different.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>As for the language barrier problem, It will be no worry since the anomaly would be able to speak their language at ease, due to you know, them having an innate knowledge of the subject's past and current life.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yeah, both of these make me as a reader skeptical because it just makes the anomaly <em>too</em> perfect and helpful, which comes off more as artificial and disingenuous to me as a reader rather than heartwarming.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>I am sorry, I didn’t quite catch that. Could you please elaborate it?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What is the reason in the Foundation universe world for the radio to do what it does? Did someone make it because they wanted to talk to their grandma? Is a ghost grandma living in the radiowaves to haunt them? Some other reason?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I am planning to make “convenient online post” to be like the debut of the anomaly in the Foundation’s eyes</p>
</blockquote>
<p>My point is that this is a kind of boring initial investigation setup.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Almost as if the mysterious creator of this anomaly is trying to play games with the Foundation, and benefit them at the same time.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why would the creator want to play games with the Foundation?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>On a unrelated note, do antique radios still function normally like they do, because I thought they were just for display.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not familiar with antique radios. Although, if it only affects such radios, would there really be that many people who would be affected by such an anomaly, to the point where the Foundation would have enough cases to notice?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I only written the increased percentage in the draft, in about 35.5 percent.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not sure a qualitative value like &quot;morale&quot; would be readily quantifiable or understandable when presented as a percentage. I recommend just sticking to similarly qualitative descriptions. There's no need to force a random percentage into the writing, especially given that readers will interpret percentages differently (one person might think 35.5 is a lot statistically, another person might think it's a number too small to be worth mentioning in a report).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The last thing you said is also great for a possible Tale about this skip</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I personally am not convinced you'd be able to make a tale for this&#8212;the material I've mentioned would make more sense for a log or supplement page. Focus on writing an SCP article first.</p>
</div>
